feat(mcp): resolve a run's parent on every registration path (#259)

A run recorded its parent on 21 registrations out of 185. The lookup worked;
it existed on one of the three paths a run can be registered by. The reference
shim resolved the edge itself, out of its own marker cache, so the first-sight
registration a tool call makes and the signer -- neither of which holds a run
id -- recorded no parent, ever.

The lookup moves into the MCP, which is the only component holding the durable
session-to-run mapping. observe_session and observe_tool_call take
parent_session_id: the harness's OWN identifier, resolved here against the
marker store. It is resolved and not stored (E4) -- only the run it names
reaches the chain.

register_agent now VALIDATES a named parent instead of copying it: a run this
ledger has never held, one that has been retired, and the run itself are each
refused with nothing appended. A lapsed parent is still a parent, because
silence is not an ending (#258). The check runs inside mint, so a replay -- how
a resumed run gets its entry healed -- is never refused for a parent that has
ended since.

The signer reads a session pointer SessionStart now publishes, keyed on the
working tree and suffixed .session so SubagentStop cannot remove it with the
subagent's. A registration refused for its parent drops the parent and keeps
the commit: an edge is worth less than the work.

internal/ledger gains ChildRuns over a partial expression index (migration
0005). The index needs an IMMUTABLE wrapper because convert_from is STABLE;
the migration says why, and what that costs.

NO BACKFILL. The 164 registrations without a parent keep none: inferring one
from timing or a shared working directory is the inference E7 forbids, and a
wrong edge is permanent.

No event type, member, enum or source changes. parent_run_id already exists.

Driven by MCP-079..082 (a parent resolved on each of the three paths; a
nonexistent, retired or self-referencing parent refused with nothing appended)
and LED-040 (children-of returns the right set and is served by the index),
each watched failing first.

